"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[61645],{261645:e=>{e.exports='# Info: Schedule and compute configuration\n\n{{SCHEDULE_INTRO}}\n\n## Recommended Default Configuration\n\nFor most detection rules, start with this configuration:\n\n- **Schedule:** At least every 10 minutes\n- **Job grouping:** Automatic\n- **Enabled:** Yes\n\n**Why this works:**\n\n- 10-minute interval balances detection speed and cost\n- Automatic compute enables job grouping to reduce costs\n- Simple and effective for most detection scenarios\n- ~10 minute detection latency is acceptable for most threats\n\n**When to adjust:**\n\n- Compliance/audit requirements (daily schedules)\n- Critical threat detection (faster intervals)\n- Business-hours-only scenarios (cron schedules)\n- Cost optimization needs (longer intervals)\n\n## Example: Brute Force Detection Schedule\n\nFor a brute force detection that identifies suspicious login patterns:\n\n**Configuration:**\n\n- **Schedule:** At least every 10 minutes\n- **Job grouping:** Automatic\n- **Enabled:** Yes\n\n**Rationale:**\n\n- 10-minute latency is acceptable for brute force attacks (attacks typically span longer periods)\n- Automatic compute enables grouping with other authentication-based detections\n- Can be grouped with other rules reading from the same authentication table\n- Cost-effective while maintaining adequate detection speed\n\n**When to adjust:**\n\n- **Faster detection (5 minutes):** For high-value targets or critical systems (higher compute cost)\n- **Business hours only:** For insider threat scenarios where attacks only occur during work hours\n- **Daily checks:** For compliance reporting (acceptable 24-hour latency)\n\n## Example: Business-Hours-Only Detection\n\nFor detections that only need to run during business hours (e.g., insider threat monitoring):\n\n**Configuration:**\n\n- **Schedule:** Exactly at (cron) - Every hour from 9 AM to 5 PM, weekdays only\n- **Job grouping:** Automatic\n- **Enabled:** Yes\n\n**Cron expression:** `0 0 9-17 ? * MON-FRI`\n\n**Rationale:**\n\n- Reduces compute costs by 72% compared to 24/7 execution\n- Insider threats typically occur during business hours\n- Hourly checks provide adequate detection for this threat model\n- Automatic grouping still applies for cost efficiency\n\n## Example: Daily Compliance Check\n\nFor compliance and audit detections with acceptable 24-hour latency:\n\n**Configuration:**\n\n- **Schedule:** Exactly at (cron) - Daily at 2 AM\n- **Job grouping:** Automatic\n- **Enabled:** Yes\n\n**Cron expression:** `0 0 2 * * ?`\n\n**Rationale:**\n\n- Lowest compute cost (runs once per day)\n- 2 AM execution during off-peak hours\n- Suitable for compliance reports and audit trails\n- 24-hour detection latency is acceptable for non-urgent alerts\n\n## Schedule Types\n\n### Duration-based: "At least every [duration]"\n\nRuns at minimum the specified interval, but may run more frequently if grouped with other rules.\n\n**Configuration:**\n\n- Specify a number and unit (minutes, hours, or days)\n- Example: 10 minutes, 1 hour, 1 day\n\n**Common intervals:**\n\n- 10 minutes (recommended default)\n- 15 minutes\n- 1 hour\n- 1 day\n\n**Best for:** Most detection rules with simple interval requirements.\n\n**Behavior:** If grouped with compatible rules, may execute more frequently than specified. For example, a 15-minute rule grouped with a 10-minute rule will effectively run every 10 minutes.\n\n### Cron-based: "Exactly at [time]"\n\nRuns at specific times using cron expressions. Can be grouped with other jobs that run at the same time.\n\n**Format:** Quartz cron syntax with 6-7 fields\n\n```\nsecond minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week [year]\n```\n\n**Common patterns:**\n\n- Every 15 minutes: `0 */15 * * * ?`\n- Every hour: `0 0 * * * ?`\n- Hourly during business hours (9 AM-5 PM, weekdays): `0 0 9-17 ? * MON-FRI`\n- Daily at 2 AM: `0 0 2 * * ?`\n- Weekdays only at midnight: `0 0 0 ? * MON-FRI`\n\n**Best for:** Business-aligned schedules (business hours only, specific times, weekdays only).\n\n**Special characters:**\n\n- `*` = Any value\n- `?` = No specific value (use for day-of-month or day-of-week)\n- `-` = Range (e.g., `9-17` for 9 AM to 5 PM)\n- `,` = List (e.g., `MON,WED,FRI`)\n- `/` = Increment (e.g., `*/15` for every 15 units)\n\n{{CONTINUOUS_SCHEDULE_TYPE_SECTION}}\n\n## Job Grouping\n\nAll detection rules use serverless compute with automatic resource management. You can control how resources are allocated and whether rules are grouped for cost optimization.\n\n### Automatic (Recommended)\n\nResources are automatically optimized and grouped with compatible jobs to reduce costs.\n\n**How it works:**\n\n- System automatically identifies compatible rules\n- Groups rules that read from the same data sources\n- Aligns schedules for efficient resource sharing\n- Optimizes compute allocation based on workload\n\n**Use for:** Most detection rules to minimize costs and maximize efficiency.\n\n**Benefits:**\n\n- Lowest compute costs through automatic grouping\n- No manual configuration needed\n- Automatically adapts as you add more rules\n\n### Dedicated\n\nThe rule runs in isolation without grouping.\n\n**How it works:**\n\n- Rule gets its own compute resources\n- No sharing with other rules\n- Consistent performance regardless of other workloads\n\n**Use when:**\n\n- High-priority rules need guaranteed consistent performance\n- Processing very large datasets that can\'t tolerate shared resources\n- Specific compliance requirements for isolated execution\n\n**Trade-off:** Higher compute costs compared to automatic grouping.\n\n### Assign to Job Group\n\nManually specify which job group this rule should join for coordinated execution.\n\n**How to configure:**\n\n- Provide a job group name (e.g., `security-detection-group`, `privilege-escalation-detections`)\n- All rules in the same group execute together\n\n**Use when:**\n\n- You need to organize related detections\n- Want manual control over which rules run together\n- Have organizational requirements for detection grouping\n- Need to coordinate scheduling across specific rules\n\n**Benefits:**\n\n- Full control over grouping behavior\n- Can organize by threat type, data source, or business function\n- Easier to manage related detections as a unit\n\n### Job Grouping\n\n**Rules can be grouped when they:**\n\n- Consume data from the same Gold table\n- Have compatible scheduling settings\n- Use similar compute resource requirements\n\n**Benefits of grouping:**\n\n- **Cost reduction:** Multiple rules share compute resources\n- **Better resource utilization:** Fewer cold starts, more efficient execution\n- **Automatic optimization:** With `automatic` mode, system handles grouping\n\n**Example:**\n\n- Rule A: Reads from `authentication` table, runs every 10 minutes\n- Rule B: Reads from `authentication` table, runs every 10 minutes\n- Result: Both rules can be grouped, sharing compute and reducing costs\n\n## Key Concepts Explained\n\n### Why the 10-minute default works\n\n**Detection latency trade-off:**\n\n- **5 minutes:** Fast detection, ~3x compute cost compared to 10 minutes\n- **10 minutes:** Good balance, reasonable detection speed and cost (recommended)\n- **30 minutes:** Lower cost, but delays threat detection\n- **1 hour:** Significant cost savings, acceptable for low-priority threats\n\n**Most security threats develop over longer timeframes:**\n\n- Brute force attacks: Typically span 15-60 minutes\n- Data exfiltration: Often occurs over hours or days\n- Insider threats: Usually have extended timelines\n- Lateral movement: Takes time to progress through network\n\n**10-minute detection latency** provides adequate response time for most threats while keeping compute costs reasonable.\n\n### When to use different schedule types\n\n**Use duration-based when:**\n\n- You want simple interval execution (every N minutes/hours/days)\n- Cost optimization through automatic grouping is important\n- You don\'t need precise control over execution times\n- Standard detection scenarios (most common)\n\n**Use cron-based when:**\n\n- Specific execution times are required\n- Business-hours-only detection saves significant costs\n- Need to align with business operations (daily reports, weekly reviews)\n- Precise scheduling control is important\n\n{{CONTINUOUS_WHEN_TO_USE_SECTION}}\n\n### Cost implications\n\n**Frequency impact:**\n\n- 5-minute interval: ~3x cost vs 10-minute (6 executions/hour vs 12)\n- 10-minute interval: Baseline (6 executions/hour)\n- 30-minute interval: ~1/3 cost vs 10-minute (2 executions/hour)\n- 1-hour interval: ~1/6 cost vs 10-minute (1 execution/hour)\n- Daily: Lowest cost (1 execution/day)\n\n**Grouping impact:**\n\n- Automatic grouping: Can reduce costs by 50-80% when multiple rules share resources\n- Dedicated: Higher cost but guaranteed performance\n- Manual groups: Cost depends on group composition\n\n**Business-hours-only impact:**\n\n- Running only M-F, 9 AM-5 PM reduces compute time by ~72% compared to 24/7\n- Significant cost savings for threat types that only occur during business hours\n\n## Best Practices\n\n### Start with the default, adjust as needed\n\nAlways begin with the recommended default (10 minutes, automatic compute) and only adjust when you have specific requirements.\n\n**Common valid reasons to adjust:**\n\n- Compliance/audit requirements (daily schedules)\n- Critical threat requiring faster detection (5-minute intervals)\n- Cost optimization for lower-priority threats (30-minute or hourly)\n- Business-hours-only threat models (cron schedules)\n\n### Match schedule to threat timeline\n\nConsider how quickly the threat develops:\n\n- **Fast-moving threats** (active attacks, data exfiltration): 5-10 minute intervals\n- **Standard threats** (brute force, privilege escalation): 10-15 minute intervals\n- **Slow-moving threats** (insider threats, compliance): Hourly or daily checks\n\n### Use cron for business-aligned detection\n\nIf a threat only occurs during specific times, use cron to save costs:\n\n- **Business hours only:** Insider threats, unauthorized access during work hours\n- **Off-hours only:** Detecting activity when it shouldn\'t occur\n- **Daily summaries:** Compliance checks, audit reports\n\n### Present configuration in plain language\n\nWhen proposing schedule configuration to users, use descriptive language instead of technical YAML:\n\n✅ **Good:**\n\n> "I\'ll configure this to run every 10 minutes with automatic compute optimization. This provides ~10 minute detection latency and is cost-effective through automatic job grouping."\n\n❌ **Avoid:**\n\n> "I\'ll set `schedule: duration`, `duration: 10`, `durationUnit: m`, `computeResource: automatic`"\n\nOnly show YAML format if user explicitly requests it.\n\n### Document adjustments from defaults\n\nIf recommending a non-default configuration, explain why:\n\n> "For this critical data exfiltration detection, I recommend 5-minute intervals (faster than the default 10 minutes) to enable rapid response. This will have higher compute costs but provides earlier threat detection."\n\n## Common Mistakes to Avoid\n\n{{CONTINUOUS_MISTAKE_SECTION}}\n\n### ❌ Setting intervals too short without justification\n\n**Problem:** Using 1-minute or 5-minute intervals for all rules.\n\n**Impact:**\n\n- Unnecessarily high compute costs\n- No practical benefit for slow-moving threats\n- Resource contention\n\n**Solution:** Start with 10-minute default. Only use shorter intervals for critical threats where faster detection provides clear business value.\n\n### ❌ Not leveraging automatic grouping\n\n**Problem:** Setting all rules to dedicated compute.\n\n**Impact:**\n\n- Missing 50-80% cost reduction from grouping\n- Inefficient resource utilization\n- Higher overall costs\n\n**Solution:** Use automatic compute for most rules. Reserve dedicated for truly high-priority cases requiring isolation.\n\n### ❌ Showing YAML to users by default\n\n**Problem:** Presenting configuration in technical YAML format.\n\n**Impact:**\n\n- Confusing for users unfamiliar with YAML\n- Makes simple changes seem complex\n- Poor user experience\n\n**Solution:** Present configuration in plain language with bullet points. Only show YAML if user explicitly requests it.\n\n### ❌ Invalid cron expressions\n\n**Problem:** Using incorrect cron syntax (e.g., wrong field count, invalid characters).\n\n**Impact:**\n\n- Configuration fails\n- Rule won\'t execute\n- User must manually fix\n\n**Solution:** Validate cron expressions before proposing. Use established patterns for common scenarios (business hours, daily, etc.).\n'}}]);
